  "id": "DrugBank_DB01260 - MONDO_6570",
  "verbalization": "Desonide, a corticosteroid, is implicated in the development of Lichen Disease through a mechanism involving prednisone metabolism. The evidence suggests that Desonide, through its indication of Neurodermatitis and Occupational Dermatitis, is linked to Prednicarbate, a corticosteroid with a well-documented role in treating Lichen Disease. The shared pathway involves the use of prednisone analogs, which are known to modulate immune responses and reduce inflammation. The final scores from the three paths indicate a moderate level of confidence in the relationship, suggesting that Desonide may contribute to the pathogenesis of Lichen Disease via a mechanism that involves the systemic effects of corticosteroids on immune regulation and skin physiology. The recurring use of prednisone analogs in the indications supports a mechanistic link, though the exact molecular pathways remain to be fully elucidated.",
